                <pb facs="0637" n="625" sameAs="frus:frus1940v05/pg625"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/4296: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Brazil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Caffery</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Rio de
                                Janeiro</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-06-29T20:00:00-03:00">June 29, 1940—8
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received June 30—7:54 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>318. My telegram no. 313, June 28, 5 p.m. Vargas insisted on answering and taking
                    issue with his critics in the United States. His speech this afternoon, although
                    reaffirming and stressing Pan American solidarity and necessity for united front
                    against foreign threats, was petulant and directed directly against those
                    critics who, he said “had interpreted his address of June 11 with false
                    commentary and tendentious publication of isolated phrases.” He asserted right
                    of each country to adopt form of government best suited to it <pb facs="0638"
                        n="626" xml:id="pg626"/> and contended that Brazil had always understood
                    Monroe Doctrine in that manner.</p>
                <p>Associated Press has telegraphed principal paragraphs. I assume that Department
                    will see papers carrying them in full.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
